---
const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Find the bundle',
    body: 'Looks inside the .exe, .app or Linux binary for the section where Bun keeps the app.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Cut away the binary',
    body: 'Walks through the files in that section and works out where the code stops and the data starts.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Write the JavaScript',
    body: 'Saves the result exactly as it found it, so nothing is quietly changed on the way out.',
  },
];

const tree = `output/claude/claude.js              all the JavaScript
output/claude/manifest.json          what is in each file
output/claude/modules/               one file per part
output/claude/sources/               the original source
output/claude/assets/                images and other files
output/claude/claude.bytecode-*.bin  the code Bun compiled`;
---

<section class="section" id="what">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>One file in.<br />JavaScript out.</h2>
      <p>
        unbuned is a single Python file. You point it at an executable, and it gives you back the
        JavaScript that app was built from. It does not decompile anything, because it does not have to:
        the code is already in there, just hidden behind a section header and a lot of binary noise.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="three">
      {steps.map((s) => (
        <div class="plain">
          <h3>{s.title}</h3>
          <p>{s.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="cap">Ask for everything with <code>--all</code>, and you get the whole tree:</p>
    <pre>{tree}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .three {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 32px;
    margin-bottom: 44px;
  }

  .cap {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .cap code {
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1px 6px;
  }
</style>
